  <!-- ── FAQ ───────────────────────────────────────────────── -->
  <section class="msv__section">
    <div class="msv__container--narrow">
      <div class="msv__section-hd">
        <h2>Frequently Asked Questions — Miami / Florida Applicants</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="msv__faq-list">
        <details>
          <summary>Can Puerto Rico residents apply for the Spain NLV through Miami?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. Puerto Rico and the US Virgin Islands fall under the jurisdiction of the Consulate General of Spain in Miami. US citizens residing in Puerto Rico or the USVI apply at the Miami consulate. You will need to travel to Coral Gables for your in-person appointment — there is no Spanish consulate in Puerto Rico or the USVI for this visa type. Your US passport is the travel document used for the application.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Does my Florida retirement income qualify for the Spain NLV?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. Florida retirement income — Social Security, Florida Retirement System (FRS) pensions, private pensions, 401(k) / IRA distributions, investment income, and annuities — all qualify as passive income for the Non-Lucrative Visa, provided the amounts are regular and recurring.</p>
            <p>For 2026, a single applicant needs approximately $2,600/month and a couple approximately $4,000/month. Most Florida retirees with a combination of Social Security and one other income source easily meet this threshold.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Which Southeast states use the Miami consulate?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>The Consulate General of Spain in Miami covers: Florida, Georgia, South Carolina, North Carolina, Tennessee, Alabama, Mississippi, Puerto Rico, and the US Virgin Islands. Applicants from Atlanta, Charlotte, Nashville, Birmingham, and other Southeast cities all apply at the Miami consulate in Coral Gables.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Do I need to translate my documents into Spanish even at the Miami consulate?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. Despite Miami's large bilingual population and the consulate being staffed by Spanish speakers, the formal application process requires certified Spanish translations of required documents — particularly the FBI background check and medical certificate. Use a professional translation service with experience in Spanish immigration documents.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I keep my Florida home while living in Spain on the NLV?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. The NLV does not require you to sell your US property or close US accounts. Many applicants retain their Florida home and divide time between Spain and the US. However, if you spend more than 183 days per year in Spain, you may become a Spanish tax resident — which has significant tax implications. This is an important conversation to have with a tax specialist before applying.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
        <details>
          <summary>Can I use income from a Florida investment property to qualify?</summary>
          <div class="msv__faq-answer">
            <p>Yes. Rental income from a Florida property qualifies as passive income for the NLV. You'll need to document it with Schedule E from your most recent Form 1040, current lease agreements showing the rental amounts, and bank statements confirming the rental deposits. Variable or seasonal rental income (like vacation rentals) may be harder to document — a consistent 12-month rental income with clear records is much stronger.</p>
          </div>
        </details>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

